These three mathematical components inject realistic human psychology and structural market mechanics into synthetic data generation:
Exponential Bubbles and Crashes: Rather than moving in a steady, linear line, the price trajectory is governed by exponential functions (A e^{kx} or A e^{-kx}). This compounding math forces the rate of change to accelerate continuously, perfectly mirroring the feedback loops of FOMO (fear of missing out) in meme stock rallies or cascading liquidations during a panic sell-off.
Volatility Regimes (Clustering): This process simulates macro-level structural shifts by manipulating the sequence's baseline noise. Instead of applying uniform randomness throughout, a step function partitions the timeline. A sequence might begin with tight, low-variance fluctuations (representing a calm, consolidated market) before abruptly transitioning into massive, high-variance swings. This teaches the model to dynamically adjust its predictions when the underlying rules of the market shift.
Momentum (Autoregressive Patterns): This introduces time-dependent memory into the price action. Using an AR(1) equation (x_t = c +  q*x_{t-1} + epsilon_t), every new data point is mathematically tethered to the one immediately preceding it. If the previous step was positive, the next step is statistically weighted to continue upward, simulating the algorithmic and institutional trend-following behavior that creates sustained market momentum.